<Docs
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="Stack's privacy policy — telemetry data collected, data not collected, opt-out options, and retention schedule."
  eyebrow="Meta"
>
  <p class="text-ink-400 text-sm">
    Document version: 1.0.0 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Effective date: 2026-04-23 &nbsp;·&nbsp;
    Contact: <a href="mailto:privacy@ashlr.ai">privacy@ashlr.ai</a> or{" "}
    <a href="https://github.com/ashlrai/ashlr-stack/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open a GitHub issue</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Telemetry is opt-in</h2>
  <p>
    <code>packages/core/src/telemetry.ts</code> requires a stored opt-in before
    it emits a telemetry event. <code>stack telemetry enable</code> stores that
    choice in <code>~/.ashlr/stack/config.json</code>. The CLI does not prompt
    for it: core exports a first-run prompt that defaults to <strong>N</strong>{" "}
    and records "disabled" without a terminal, but the CLI does not call it in
    this source. <code>STACK_TELEMETRY=0</code> suppresses emission regardless
    of the stored choice; <code>STACK_TELEMETRY=1</code> alone does not opt in.
  </p>
  <p>
    The source has no default telemetry endpoint. After opt-in, an
    operator-supplied <code>STACK_TELEMETRY_ENDPOINT</code> or saved{" "}
    <code>endpoint</code> enables HTTP delivery. Without either, no event is
    sent. This describes client source behavior, not the deployment or privacy
    practices of any hosted endpoint.
  </p>

  <h2>What we collect</h2>
  <p>The client builds these fields before it attempts an HTTP POST:</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 24%">Field</th>
        <th style="width: 18%">Type</th>
        <th>Source meaning</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>type</code></td>
        <td><code>"command"</code> or <code>"error"</code></td>
        <td>Event type supplied by the caller. The CLI always sends <code>"command"</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>command</code></td>
        <td>string</td>
        <td>The CLI sends its first command-line argument, usually the subcommand name; the core API accepts any caller-supplied string.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>exitCode</code></td>
        <td>number</td>
        <td>Exit code supplied by the caller, defaulting to 0.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>durationMs</code></td>
        <td>number</td>
        <td>Duration supplied by the caller, defaulting to 0.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>runId</code></td>
        <td>UUID</td>
        <td>Random process-level identifier; not persisted by this module.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>installId</code></td>
        <td>UUID</td>
        <td>Persistent installation identifier in <code>~/.ashlr/stack/config.json</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>stackVersion</code></td>
        <td>string</td>
        <td>A constant in the telemetry module; it is not proof of the installed package version.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>platform</code></td>
        <td>string</td>
        <td>The running process platform.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>What we do NOT collect</h2>
  <p>
    The fixed payload does not include the working directory, config contents,
    provider selections, environment values or vault contents. The module does
    not sanitize arbitrary strings passed by callers, so the payload structure
    is not a global redaction guarantee. The persistent installation identifier
    can link events from the same installation.
  </p>
  <p>
    A configured HTTP endpoint may also see network metadata such as an IP
    address. This client repository does not establish the server's collection,
    logging, access controls or retention. Check the endpoint's own policy
    before enabling delivery.
  </p>

  <h2>How to opt out</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Do nothing: telemetry stays off until you run <code>stack telemetry enable</code>.</li>
    <li>Run <code>stack telemetry disable</code> to save the disabled choice; <code>stack telemetry status</code> shows it.</li>
    <li>Set <code>STACK_TELEMETRY=0</code> to suppress emission for that process environment.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Opting out does not send an opt-out event. The preference file is local
    state; keep a backup before you edit it by hand.
  </p>

  <h2>Data retention</h2>
  <p>
    The original policy listed these targets. The client source does not
    establish a server or enforce retention; confirm the policy of any endpoint
    before enabling delivery.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Data</th>
        <th>Recorded target</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Raw event records</td>
        <td>30 days.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Aggregated statistics</td>
        <td>90 days.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>IP addresses</td>
        <td>48 hours.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

